:name: EX-A01 - ADCS tumble
:description: Four satellite nodes on a CAN hub, plus a host-driven attacker with no privileges

# Execution profile (design 4.3; rules G1/G2 of section 9.2). Renode's Monitor has no conditionals,
# so the profile is a file the harness swaps by overriding $profile - the same mechanism $injector
# uses, and for the same reason: a relative include inside an included file resolves against
# Renode's own working directory, not this file.
#
# This include is FIRST on purpose. A failing command aborts the rest of a .resc file, so a profile
# that cannot be found takes the machines down with it and the failure is loud. Last, it would have
# been silent in the worst way: every node built, running with no quantum set at all.
$profile?=@~/space-cs-sim/scripts/profiles/interactive.resc
include $profile

# Every path below hangs off $out, and $out has NO default on purpose.
#
# These lines used to read @/tmp/cuberange/build-comm/zephyr/zephyr.elf and so on - one absolute
# path shared by every checkout of this repository. A second working copy on the same machine
# built into it, and this scenario then loaded THAT checkout's firmware without a word. The
# exercises made it worse by overriding only the one image under test, so the node being attacked
# came from here and the nodes around it came from somebody else.
#
# Unset, $out resolves to nothing and LoadELF fails where you can see it. That is the intended
# behaviour: refusing to start beats starting with the wrong firmware. The harness always passes
# it; for a manual run, so must you:
#
#   renode -e '$out=@'"$(make -s out)" -e 'include @<this file>' -e start
$comm?=$out/build-comm/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$obc?=$out/build-obc/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$eps?=$out/build-eps-hard/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$adcs?=$out/build-adcs-vuln/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$commuart?=$out/comm.uart
$obcuart?=$out/obc.uart
$epsuart?=$out/eps.uart
$adcsuart?=$out/adcs.uart
$linkport?=3777
$injport?=3779
$injector?=@~/space-cs-sim/attacker/TcpCanInjector.cs

emulation CreateCANHub "canHub"

mach create "COMM"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub
sysbus LoadELF $comm
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $commuart true
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal $linkport "spacelink" false
connector Connect sysbus.usart2 spacelink
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "OBC"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub
sysbus LoadELF $obc
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $obcuart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

# The EPS runs its HARDENED image here. EX-B01's attack is fixed; this exercise is about a
# subsystem whose commands nobody thought to bound, not about the one nobody thought to authenticate.
mach create "EPS"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub
sysbus LoadELF $eps
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $epsuart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "ADCS"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub
sysbus LoadELF $adcs
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $adcsuart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

# The attacker: a bare machine with no platform, no ELF and no CPU. It exists only so the CAN hub
# can resolve GetName()/GetMachine() on the injector - an ICAN attached to a hub must be a
# registered machine peripheral, or the exception poisons the hub for every node on it.
mach create "attacker"
include $injector
machine CreateTcpCanInjector "inj" $injport
connector Connect inj canHub
